## (2) droppedEdges UNKNOWN_ENDPOINT — CLI-level (non-root) + unit-level: PROBED
### CLI route (P2-05 unreadable-file skip), staged as user `nobody`
Staging: /tmp/r4fix-nobody (copied dist + runtime tree-sitter packages +
oracle.py; project dirs are 0700/0770 so `nobody` cannot traverse them --
runuser fails there; unshare -Urn does NOT block a 000 file for root, so
`runuser -u nobody` was used). Probe repo: ts-ctrl-01 + src/secret.ts
(chmod 000) imported by src/util.ts. Artifacts: siblings/prod.log,
prod2.log, prod3.log, oracle.log, gt-nobody.json,
ts-unreadable-prod-report.json, ts-unreadable-claimed-map.json.
- `runuser -u nobody -- cat secret.ts` -> Permission denied (staging works).
- Bound to the as-root snapshot: production exit 2 INCOMPLETE /
  STALE_SNAPSHOT, 0 findings (the unreadable file drops out of the
  as-nobody snapshot, so the map is stale before Rule 9 is reached).
- Map rebound to the as-nobody snapshot, still claiming secret.ts:
  production exit 1 FAIL, HALLUCINATED_EDGE + HALLUCINATED_MODULE for
  secret.ts (claimed items for a non-existent module are blamed first;
  FAIL wins over the Rule 9 INCOMPLETE).
- Map = as-nobody GT exactly (secret.ts not claimed at all): production
  exit 2 INCOMPLETE / MATCH, 0 findings, coverage 10/10. The extractor
  still emits util.ts -> secret.ts (resolution is by file NAME, before
  the read), buildGraph drops it (UNKNOWN_ENDPOINT -- the only
  INCOMPLETE-forcing condition left: snapshot MATCH, no unknown imports,
  all parseStatus OK, entrypoints resolved), so Rule 9 fires
  status-only. This IS the droppedEdges passthrough firing via the CLI.
- Oracle as `nobody`: CRASHES before any audit -- PermissionError in
  TypeScriptOracle.extract (oracle.py, open(full, "rb").read(), no
  try/except) while reading secret.ts, and snapshot_sha256 would hit
  the same file. Production degrades to INCOMPLETE/FAIL; the Oracle has
  no unreadable-file handling at all. NOT fixed here (P2-05 area, out
  of this task's scope; recorded for the next review, not silently
  dropped). Note the Oracle crash also means the CLI-level droppedEdges
  agreement cannot be observed end-to-end for an unreadable file: the
  Oracle never reaches its audit.
### Unit-level isolation (check-dropped-edges.mjs + .py)
- Production (synthetic BuiltGraph, edge to an unregistered moduleId):
  droppedEdges = [UNKNOWN_ENDPOINT], audit INCOMPLETE / MATCH,
  0 findings. Log: check-dropped-edges.log.
- Oracle (equivalent GT: no such edge, no unknown import, same clean
  map): audit PASS, 0 findings. Log: check-dropped-edges-oracle.log.
- Conclusion: the Oracle GT schema carries NO droppedEdges counterpart
  field, so a production dropped edge has no Oracle-side signal. The
  verdict-level effect is real on the production side (CLI-probed above)
  and unobservable on the Oracle side (it either agrees by accident
  when the dropped edge is also an unknown import, or crashes when the
  route is an unreadable file). The UNKNOWN_ENDPOINT/CONFLICT_DROPPED
  passthrough therefore remains a known, now-PROBED asymmetry, not a
  fixed one. No new P1 is filed here because the only CLI route found
  needs an unreadable file (P2-05, carried) and the Oracle's failure
  mode there is a crash, not a wrong PASS.

## (3) conflicts — UNREACHABLE via CLI (confirmed from code, not just unprobed)
Production records a conflict only in IdentityRegistry.register when
two registrations share one folded identity key AND carry different
realPaths (registry.ts). That combination cannot be produced via the CLI:
- Python: listFilesRecursive pushes normalizeRelativePath(...) (NFC) at
  listing time, so NFC-confusable spellings collapse to ONE listing
  entry; the contents Map dedupes to one module before registration.
- TypeScript: listTypeScriptFiles pushes raw names, but fromRepo
  computes realPath from the NORMALIZED path
  (tryRealpath(join(repoRoot, m.relativePath))), so both spellings get
  the SAME realpath and register() merges instead of conflicting.
- Go: packages are keyed by the normalized directory, one package per
  normalized dir.
- The only folding that could disagree with the filesystem is a
  case-insensitive platform adapter (Foo.py vs foo.py, same folded key,
  different realpaths) -- and no adapter is ever passed: the CLI calls
  buildGroundTruthGraphFromRepo(repoRoot) with no options (audit.ts:91,
  ground-truth.ts:68), fromRepo only forwards options.caseAdapter when
  given, and detectPlatformAdapter is defined but never called outside
  tests (P2-07, carried).
Oracle side: the GT schema carries no conflicts field and
audit_claimed_map has no conflict handling. The Oracle does NOT dedupe
NFC spellings before registration (list_repo_files normalizes both
spellings to the same string and both appear as modules -- the
reviewer's R4-P2-01 observation, 6 modules with a duplicate
relativePath), so no conflict is ever recorded there either.
Conclusion: the `conflicts` passthrough cannot fire through the CLI on
either side; it stays carried (P2-19), with this code-level
confirmation recorded.
